# Dates from engine/notation/chronicle.ts: Ashoka ruled about 268 to 232 BC; Brahmagupta wrote zero
# as a number in 628; Fibonacci's Liber Abaci brought the digits to Europe in 1202.
lesson history-ashoka-and-the-digits v=1 format=teach grade=4 unit=3 subject=history levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "India: Ashoka, and the digits we write"
  goal "Say how the digits we write travelled from India to the world, and read a king's carved words as a first-hand source."

  look {
    scene 36x9 {
      timeline line from=-300 to=1500 step=300 years=[-268, 628, 1202] labels=["Ashoka's rule", "Zero as a number", "Digits reach Europe"] at=canvas(0, 0)
    }
    say """
      About 268 BC Ashoka became emperor of most of India. He had his words carved on rocks and
      pillars across the land, telling people to be kind and fair, and his carvings hold some of the
      oldest number signs we know. Indian mathematicians went on to use ten digits with a place for
      each, and in 628 Brahmagupta wrote about zero as a number. The digits travelled west, and a
      book of 1202 brought them to Europe.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked history.numeral-letters k=0
    }
    show history.numeral-letters k=2 easy-k=1 hard-k=4
    show history.digits-order k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.digits-between k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.edict-first-hand k=0 hard-k=1
    show history.numeral-letters k=3 easy-k=2 hard-k=5
    show history.digits-order k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show history.digits-between k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show history.edict-first-hand k=1 hard-k=0
    show history.roman-numerals k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show history.why-zero k=0 hard-k=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show history.digit-years k=0 hard-k=1
  }
  remember {
    say "The ten digits and the zero came from India, and they won because every place has its own digit."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is that the numbers we write, ten digits whose places give their value, with zero to
    hold an empty place, were worked out in India and travelled west over a thousand years. The way
    in counts the letters a year needs in Roman numerals; the core orders the digits' journey,
    counts years across year one, and reads Ashoka's carved edicts as a first-hand source (the
    king's own words) against a history written long after. The wrong idea to watch for is "zero
    is nothing, so it does not matter", which the two-star question tests. If the child is stuck,
    write 105 and 15 on cards and ask what the 0 does. Off the page, find the oldest date you can
    on a building near you and write it both ways. England's key stage 2 history asks for "a
    non-European society that provides contrasts with British history"; Russia's history
    programme for grade five includes «Держава Маурьев» and «Культурное наследие Древней Индии
    (... научное познание)», the Mauryan empire and ancient India's learning.
    Japan's 社会 takes up history only through Japan's own story, in grade six
    (「我が国の歴史上の主な事象について，人物の働きや代表的な文化遺産を中心に」, the course of study as read in its 2008 edition), so
    this lesson has no line of its own there; its timeline and source skills serve that grade.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level a worked example counts the letters of MMXXVI, the numerals are short, the digits' journey comes nearly in order, and the years counted stay after year one."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the numerals are long years such as MDCCCLXXXVIII, the journey is fully muddled, the years counted cross year one, and the three-star puzzle asks which years come after AD 300."
  }
}
